**(2) The bug-introducing change:** Commit `b83ce94c` (Sep 18 2015) refactored the original code:
```java
if (nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove) {
  DefaultSoundChannel.playSoundOnLocalMachine(SoundPath.CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_NONCOMBAT, id.getName());
  m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove = true;
} else if (!nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove) {
  DefaultSoundChannel.playSoundOnLocalMachine(SoundPath.CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_COMBAT, id.getName());
  m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove = true;
}
```
into:
```java
if ((nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove) || (!nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove)) {
  ClipPlayer.play(SoundPath.CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_NONCOMBAT, id.getName());
  m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove = true;
}
```
This merge dropped the combat-move branch entirely: during a combat move, the wrong sound (`CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_NONCOMBAT` instead of `CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_COMBAT`) is played, and `m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove` is never set to `true`, so the (wrong) sound would also replay on every subsequent combat move. This is the exact bug the fix commit addresses (restoring two independent `if` blocks and the missing flag assignment — though the fix still leaves the combat branch playing `CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_NONCOMBAT` rather than `CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_COMBAT`, which appears to be a separate, pre-existing/unaddressed issue not in scope here).

**(3) Supporting evidence:** The `git log -p` history shows a clean, unbroken lineage for this exact block of code: original separate if/else-if (pre-2015) → combined OR condition introduced by `b83ce94c` → combined condition persisted unchanged through subsequent commits (confirmed via `23b6ecce5`, which only touched an unrelated line) → finally split back into two `if` blocks with the missing flag restored by `9a02e14e`. No other commit touched this logic in between, making `b83ce94c` the sole origin of the defect.
